f the segment in factorized form (only useful primes), as in normal form its enormous. The power of some prime in this form is the sum of powers of this prime in all the numbers in the segment. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We firstly move the left border of the segment one step to the right and then keep moving the right border to the right until power of at least one prime number in the product is smaller than in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". It means that it is not divisible by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Moving the left border means subtracting all the powers of useful primes of number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" from the product and moving the right border is adding all the powers of useful primes of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first time we reach such a segment, we add ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") to answer (consider "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" 0-indexed)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overall complexity: "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/35937476d572c480d8a6153ae2cfda271e96686c0c8db72056845d4de33ac06b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"MAXN"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is up to 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"9"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"818F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/818/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"818F - Level Generation"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The best way to build a graph is to make a 2-edge-connected component with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" vertices and connect each of the remaining "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" vertices to it with a single edge. Then we will have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" bridges outside the component and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e66a2722d493eebc142a91c9894097d8d204ce384555c151458a6048c692c98b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" edges in the component. So the answer for some fixed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f0ed8a51b99a67c7ec78f683f2fa09a0476da20d1995f00c1e71d5effe1cc538.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"; let's denote is at "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now since "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/83a0c16f9e4c4a493b58a0307e642f47fc95b99f7b33834829dbb2aa2fccc831.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" is increasing, and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is decreasing, there exists some "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", then "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ff6d7f9b453c61387294d488c8a7eb79b3aaa392f7c4d09ed57c9e493ca653a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", and if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", then "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/64ddba5b5bce4fb4ee7edd4b5db5d0ee43ee73ddb277ae332eef10f8d8a5a4e9.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is strictly increasing on the segment [1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"], and strictly decreasing on the segment ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]; and this proves that we can use ternary search to find its maximum. "}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"818G"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/818/problem/G"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"818G - Four Melodies"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's build a directed graph where vertices represent notes and a directed edge comes from vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" iff "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be consecutive notes in a melody. Now we have to find four longest vertex-disjoint paths in this graph."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This problem can be solved with mincost "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-flow algorithms. We build a network where each vertex of the graph is split into two (let's denote the vertices that we obtain when we are splitting some vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Then each directed edge "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/aee5be3e3a58c8c2360bcc13b18ffed898b0b40dd4f3c7302beea9eb652cc381.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" transforms into a directed edge from vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" to vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the network, the capacity of this edge is 1, and the cost is 0. Also we add directed edges from the source to every vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and from every vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" to the sink (they have the same characteristics: capacity is 1, cost is 0). And for each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we add a directed edge between "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"; these edges actually represent that we are using some note in a melody, so their capacities are also equal to 1, and their costs are  - 1. The answer to the problem is equal to the absolute value of minimum cost of 4-flow in this network."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The bad thing is that the network is really large. So we have to use some advanced mincost algorithm here."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Model solution uses Dijkstra's algorithm with Johnson's potentials to find augmenting paths of minimum cost. We set a number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") for each vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of the network (these numbers are called potentials). Then we modify costs of the edges: if some edge "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/aee5be3e3a58c8c2360bcc13b18ffed898b0b40dd4f3c7302beea9eb652cc381.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" had cost "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", now it's cost is {"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'}"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). It's easy to prove that if some path from vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" was the shortest one between these vertices without modifying the costs with potentinals, then after modifying it will also be the shortest between these vertices. So instead of looking for an augmenting path in the original network we can try looking for it in a network with modified edges. Why? Because it is always possible to set all potentials in such a way that all costs of edges will be non-negative (and we will be able to use Dijkstra to find the shortest path from the source to the sink)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Before looking for the first augmenting path, we calculate potentials recursively: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"source"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = 0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"{"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"} (we check all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s such that there is an edge "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/9f8920fc9759584e3084a0756b58fd9706ad9eb88644e35e38f1268a4fdf0719.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the network). The network is acyclic before we push flow, so there is always a way to calculate these potentials with dynamic programming. Then each time we want to find an augmenting path, we run Dijkstra's algoritm on modified network, push flow through the path we found and modify the potentials: new potential of each vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" becomes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is the distance between the source and vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the modified network (and we found this distance with Dijkstra). When we have found four augmenting paths, we are done and it's time to evaluate the cost of the flow."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/52991"}